Kernir patch 0015: the xar reader refuses deep, long or oversized TOCs while it parses them

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read_support_format_xar.c (read_toc, file_new, xml_data,
expat_end_cb, expat_read_toc). Applies on top of 0001-0014 (touches no file they touch).

read_toc inflates and parses the whole XML TOC before archive_read_next_header returns the first entry. It
builds an entry for every <file> and copies each entry's parent pathname into its own, so the stored
pathnames grow with the square of the depth, and then copies the original's pathname into every hardlink.
No limit in the caller comes in time: a 14 KB xar of 4,000 nested folders with 255-byte names cost
2.6 GB and 12-21 s; 5,000 hardlinks to a 65,000-byte pathname cost 325 MB of copies.

While the TOC is parsed:
- An entry nested more than KERNIR_XAR_DEPTH (200) levels deep is refused in file_new, before it is
  allocated. Folders with only a name and a type are never listed, but they nest and cost the same, so
  they count too.
- A pathname (after the parent's is prepended) or a symlink target over KERNIR_XAR_NAME_BYTES (65,536)
  bytes is refused in xml_data; a link target before it is copied.
- The stored pathnames (full, parent prefix included) and symlink targets are added up, and a total over
  KERNIR_XAR_NAMES_BYTES (256 MiB) is refused; the hardlink connect loop in read_toc charges each
  original's pathname once per hardlink before it copies it.
These sit at or above what Kernir lists: 200 path components, 16 KiB per path, 64 KiB per name or link
target and 256 MiB of names and link targets per archive (the limits patch 0014 is justified by). A path
over 64 KiB raw is still over 16 KiB after NFC, so the path limit refuses nothing the caller would list.
One caveat: the total sees raw TOC bytes, before the NFC of patch 0003, which can shrink decomposed text
up to threefold, so names padded with decomposed characters can be over 256 MiB here and within it after
NFC; and it charges folders the caller never sees.

Each refusal sets ARCHIVE_ERRNO_FILE_FORMAT with its own message ("xar entry nests more than 200
directories deep", "xar entry name is longer than 65536 bytes", "xar link target is longer than 65536
bytes", "xar entry names and link targets take more than 256 MiB", "xar hardlink targets take the entry
names past 256 MiB"), returns ARCHIVE_FATAL and sets the archive format to xar (read_toc sets it only
once the TOC is read, so the refusal would otherwise read as an unknown format). With expat, a callback's
error is kept when XML_Parse later fails in the same chunk, and no end callback runs after it.
Archives within the limits read as before.

Apply from Sources/CLibArchive: patch -p1 < patches/0015-xar-toc-limits.patch

